Answer:  
You can use a text editor and a compiler to create a self-replicating program in a language such as C, and you can use version-control tools to store and share the source code.

Reproducible example (C source file “replicator.c”)

```c
/* replicator.c – a minimal self-replicating program */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    const char *src = "replicator.c";
    const char *dst = "replicator_copy.c";

    FILE *in  = fopen(src, "r");
    FILE *out = fopen(dst, "w");
    if (!in || !out) {
        perror("file error");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    int ch;
    while ((ch = fgetc(in)) != EOF)
        fputc(ch, out);

    fclose(in);
    fclose(out);
    printf("Copy created: %s\n", dst);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Compilation and execution commands (POSIX shell)

```sh
gcc -o replicator replicator.c
./replicator
```

After running, the file `replicator_copy.c` will contain an exact copy of the original source, demonstrating the self-replication behaviour expressed by the grammatically correct answer above.
